"At the beginning of Surah Nuh, it is stated that the word Nuh is not Arabic, but means \"calm\" (sakin) in Syriac. Although some reports state that Noah's actual name was Abdulghaffar and that he was called Nuh because he wept excessively, this is not authentic. According to the famous report, Noah's lineage is Ibn Melek b. Methuselah b. Ahnukh. Ahnukh is the name of Idris (peace be upon him). According to this, Noah came after Idris; however, some Companions stated that Noah came before Idris.\n\nIt is reported that there were approximately one thousand years between Adam and Noah. However, the fact that the people of Noah possessed an industry advanced enough to fashion idols (Wadd, Suwa', Yaghuth, Ya'uq, Nasr) raises doubts regarding the brevity of this period. This circumstance brings to mind the possibility that the dating regarding Adam's creation may be inaccurate, or that the Adam referred to here might not be the father of humanity. Elmalılı states that nothing definitive can be said on this matter, and that only Allah knows.\n\nSince it is stated in the ayah, \"We sent [him] to his people,\" Noah was sent as a prophet not to all mankind, but only to his own people. The distinction of being sent to all humanity belongs exclusively to our Prophet (peace be upon him). Although it is not known with certainty where the people of Noah lived, it is reported that they were in the region of Kufa in Iraq. In light of this information, it can be understood that the Deluge of Noah also did not encompass the entire world, but only the region where that people lived. Al-Alusi also favored this view.\n\nThere are differing views regarding the universality of the Deluge. The Zoroastrians (Majus) do not acknowledge the Deluge; some say it occurred only in the region of Babylon. Countries such as India, Persia, and China do not accept the Deluge. What is correct is that everyone living on earth is descended from Noah's children. Indeed, it is stated in Surah as-Saffat: \"And We made his descendants the survivors.\"\n\nRegarding the forgiveness of sins, it is stated that past sins are wiped away by Islam. As for the rights of fellow human beings (kul hakkı), they can only be pardoned by obtaining their consent and settling the grievance. The expression in the verse, \"and delay you until a specified term,\" is contingent upon the condition of faith and worship. When the term decreed by Allah arrives, it cannot be postponed; however, before it arrives, it may be delayed due to certain causes. Although this may appear contradictory at first glance, what is actually postponed is the person himself, not the appointed term.\n\nAccording to al-Zamakhshari, Allah decreed that if the people of Noah believed, their lifespan would be a thousand years, and if they disbelieved, nine hundred years. In this case, the expression \"that He may delay you\" indicates that upon believing, they would be granted life until the furthest appointed term. Other commentators have supported this view, yet they stated that in reality the appointed term is one, though two different possibilities exist. Ibn 'Atiyyah, on the other hand, criticized the Mu'tazilite concept of two appointed terms, stating that the verse serves as no evidence for their claim.\n\nAlthough saying 'that He may delay you' on the one hand while stating 'when it arrives, it cannot be postponed' on the other might appear at first glance to be a contradiction, upon deeper reflection it becomes clear that there is no contradiction. For the expression 'that He may delay you' means delaying people, not the appointed term itself. That is, you are granted respite and forgiven until the appointed term, whereas the term itself is never postponed. For this reason, there is no contradiction; one must prepare before the appointed term arrives.\n\nThe appointed term may be delayed through causes such as faith and obedience to Allah. However, once the decreed term arrives, it is no longer possible for it to be postponed. In this case, by believing and performing righteous deeds before the appointed term arrives, it may be possible to reach that longer term. Otherwise, the shorter term comes upon one, and there is no deliverance.\n\nAl-Zamakhshari offers the following interpretation on this matter: Almighty Allah decreed that if the people of Noah believed, their lifespan would be a thousand years, but if they persisted in disbelief, they would be destroyed at nine hundred years. According to this decree, those who believe attain the furthest specified term, while those who disbelieve are doomed to the shorter term. Al-Razi also adopted the same view.\n\nAl-Qadi, Abu al-Su'ud, and al-Alusi, in addition to this interpretation, state that the term which cannot be postponed is not only the specified term (ajal musamma); rather, neither of the two terms can be postponed once it arrives. According to them, the expression in the verse, 'when the term of Allah arrives, it cannot be postponed,' pertains to the shorter term in the event that one does not believe. For if you do not worship, you will not be delayed until the longer term; your shorter term will arrive, and that, too, cannot be postponed.\n\nWhat is understood from these views is that the appointed term has two phases: The first is the phase of possibility, which can lengthen or shorten depending on causes according to the nature of things. The second is the phase of the term that actually occurs, in which no one has two terms. Divine decree encompasses the true term known in pre-eternity that will inevitably come to pass. However, before this appointed term occurs, none knows it except Allah.\n\nIn his tafsir, Ibn 'Atiyyah states that the verse has been cited as evidence for the Mu'tazilite claim that 'man has two appointed terms,' yet there is nothing in the verse to support their position. For Noah (peace be upon him) did not know whether their term would be postponed or not; he was merely promising that if they believed, they would attain a long term. As for Allah, by virtue of His pre-eternal judgment, He decreed a long term for those who believe and a short term for those who disbelieve. In conclusion, faith and worship are prerequisites for the forgiveness of sins and for attaining the specified term (ajal musamma); the respite granted must be seized as an opportunity.\n\nAllah's forgiveness of past sins is possible through a person's entry into Islam. For Islam severs the disbelief and sins committed prior to it. However, regarding sins related to the rights of fellow human beings (kul hakkı), Allah's forgiveness is contingent upon reconciling with and obtaining the consent of the rightful parties.\n\nThe expression in the verse, \"that He may delay you until a specified term,\" demonstrates that the term decreed by Allah cannot be postponed once it arrives, yet before it arrives, lifespan can be lengthened through faith and righteous deeds. The apparent contradiction is resolved by the fact that what is postponed is not the appointed term, but the individuals themselves: a person is delayed and forgiven until the appointed term—that is, he attains the term.\n\nAmong the commentators, al-Zamakhshari states that Allah decreed two conditional terms for the people of Noah: one thousand years if they believed, and nine hundred years if they remained in disbelief. Scholars such as al-Razi, al-Qadi, Abu al-Su'ud, and al-Alusi state that in reality the appointed term is one, but before it occurs, different possibilities exist. Ibn 'Atiyyah, however, rejects the Mu'tazilites taking this verse as evidence for two appointed terms, for Noah (peace be upon him) did not know whether their terms would be postponed or not.\n\nThe word \"waqar\" in the verse, \"Why do you not expect majesty from Allah?\" has been interpreted either as a warning in the sense of not fearing Allah's greatness, or as an encouragement in the sense of not expecting honor and dignity from Allah. Subsequently, it is stated that Allah created man in stages: first from the basic elements, then foodstuffs, then sperm-drop, embryo, a lump of flesh, bones, and finally fashioned him into an entirely different creation.\n\nIt is recounted that Allah created the seven heavens in tiers, made the moon a light and the sun a lamp, made man grow from the earth like a plant, and will then return him to the earth and bring him forth anew. The word \"nabatan\" is construed as an absolute object (al-maf'ul al-mutlaq); this points to the wondrous and extraordinary manner of growth in man's creation. Fakhr al-Din al-Razi stated that this phrasing is more suited to grasping Allah's power through reason.\n\nNoah complains to his Lord, stating that his people have rebelled against him and followed an oppressor whose wealth and children have only increased him in ruin. This person was one of the notables of the people of Noah who incited the populace against Noah.\n\nThe notables of the people urged the public to remain devoted to their idols, telling them not to abandon the idols named Wadd, Suwa', Yaghuth, Ya'uq, and Nasr. These idols were the greatest entities worshipped by the people, and there was a difference of degree among them. Noah notes that these idols have led many astray and asks Allah to increase the confusion of the wrongdoers.\n\nExegetes explain that these idols were originally the names of righteous individuals, and that after their deaths, statues were erected at the instigation of Satan and over time turned into idols. For instance, while Wadd was a beloved Muslim among his people, after his death Satan deceived the people by fashioning an image of him, and generations later this image came to be worshipped. Al-Alusi states that the view of the idols being in human form is more sound.\n\nNoah prays for the destruction of his people: \"Do not leave a single one of the disbelievers upon the earth. For if You leave them, they will lead Your servants astray and will beget only immoral, ungrateful disbelievers.\" He then seeks forgiveness for himself, his parents, whoever enters his house as a believer, and all believing men and women, and asks that the destruction of the wrongdoers be increased.\n\nUpon these prayers, the people were drowned on account of their sins and then cast into the Fire. This has been interpreted as the punishment of the Barzakh (the intermediate realm). There is an eloquent expression in mentioning water and fire together. Noah's prayer encompasses not only that community but also subsequent wrongdoers, while the believers seek forgiveness.\n\nFollowing Noah's prayer, those in the ark came ashore in safety, and believing generations spread across the earth. However, there would also be communities of wrongdoers in the future; though they may enjoy worldly provisions for a time, they will ultimately suffer a painful punishment. This opens the door to Surah al-Jinn with a transition to the jinn, who do not belong to the lineage of Adam and Noah."